@* A card that is one big button holding smaller live controls — which a browser will not
   render: a button may not contain interactive content. So the card's own action becomes a
   full-card overlay behind everything, and the content is raised above it with pointer
   events off — the links and tiles inside declare `pointer-events-auto` (or take
   RaiseAboveOverlay, where a component offers it) and stay clickable, while everything else
   falls through to the overlay.

   The adversary record cards on the statistics page and the reference page each spelled the
   arrangement out and cross-referenced the other; this is that arrangement said once. The
   card around it stays the caller's and must carry `relative`, since the overlay pins to it.
   FilterBar's header keeps its own copy on purpose — its overlay is also the hover surface
   and a disclosure, so it carries aria-expanded and a conditional radius this one has no
   business growing. *@

<button type="button" @onclick="OnOpen" title="@Label"
        class="absolute inset-0 rounded-xl focus-ring">
    <span class="sr-only">@Label</span>
</button>
<div class="pointer-events-none relative">
    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What opening the card does — the tooltip, and the screen reader's name for
    /// the otherwise-invisible button.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback OnOpen { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
